<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uyayna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Al-Uyaynah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uyayna is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> region of central Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> in Wadi Hanifa. It was the birthplace of the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a>, who began his preaching there in the early 1740s under the protection of its ruler Uthman ibn Mu'ammar. Under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulayman-ibn-muhammad-al-hamid">Sulayman ibn Muhammad</a> of the Banu Khalid, the town expelled him, and he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a>, where he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud">Muhammad ibn Saud</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uyayna.jpg" alt="Uyayna" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: haitham alfalah · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Oyainah_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
